# A companion who dies of old age mid-visit is buried where they stand.
#
# The OldAge arm of PartyMember.CauseOfDeath. This was the ONLY death a companion could suffer before
# — CheckOldAgeDeaths removed them and raised the notice — but it did so only at the world map, and
# only for age. Folding it into SettleCompanionDeaths is what gave the other two causes a route, and
# this script is what makes sure the fold did not cost old age anything: the notice still names the
# age reached, and the body is now left as well, which it never used to be.
#
# The clock is pushed from inside the location rather than with --advance-days, for two reasons. The
# body needs an area to lie in, and there is none on the world map. And a cat outlives a person: at
# this seed Nighthunter's span runs past 48,000 days while a protagonist's heart tops out at 43,200 —
# so on the world map the protagonist's own old-age check (which runs first, and ends the run) would
# always fire before the cat's. Inside a session that check does not run, which is what leaves the
# companion's death reachable on its own.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --fill-party --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --location-type test --location-id 0 --npc-static --start-area "Test Hall" --period dawn

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

inspect party
expect-state party Nighthunter

# Well past the cat's span. `clock` is the only way to move the world clock from inside a visit —
# it otherwise advances on travel arrival and work stints alone.
clock 45000
wait 30

# The sweep runs every tick outside a fight, so the notice comes up without anything being clicked.
expect Nighthunter
expect died at

click companion-death
wait 60
advance 12 180

inspect party
expect-no-state party Nighthunter
inspect pois
expect-state pois Nighthunter's Remains

quit
